1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,325
Before the Banks: How Singapore's
Merchants Kept Their Money

2
00:00:04,325 --> 00:00:07,937
For its first twenty-one
years, Singapore was one of

3
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:11,850
the busiest free ports in
Asia and had no bank at all.

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:15,871
Its merchants still borrowed,
lent, paid and kept their money,

5
00:00:15,951 --> 00:00:19,111
through the trading firms
known as agency houses,

6
00:00:19,191 --> 00:00:24,350
through moneylenders, and through credit
extended from one trader to the next.

7
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:28,314
When the first bank finally
opened a branch in 1840,

8
00:00:28,394 --> 00:00:32,050
it joined that system
rather than replacing it.

9
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:36,439
In the port's early years, the
work that a bank would do was done

10
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:40,976
by the agency houses, European
trading firms such as Guthrie & Co.

11
00:00:41,056 --> 00:00:45,525
that bought and sold goods on
commission for merchants elsewhere.

12
00:00:45,525 --> 00:00:49,945
They held money for their clients,
advanced credit against goods,

13
00:00:50,025 --> 00:00:53,625
and moved funds between
Singapore, India and Europe.

14
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:58,392
According to the National Library
Board's history of money lending,

15
00:00:58,472 --> 00:01:00,779
agency houses later went further,

16
00:01:00,859 --> 00:01:05,200
financing factories, sawmills,
tin mines and rubber estates.

17
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,622
Trade itself ran on silver coins,

18
00:01:07,702 --> 00:01:12,025
mostly the Spanish dollar,
and on credit between traders.

19
00:01:12,025 --> 00:01:15,037
Goods changed hands on
the promise of payment

20
00:01:15,117 --> 00:01:18,128
after the next voyage,
and much of the port's

21
00:01:18,208 --> 00:01:21,575
business depended on who
could be trusted to pay.

22
00:01:21,575 --> 00:01:25,232
The first bank in
Singapore was a branch of

23
00:01:25,312 --> 00:01:29,050
the Union Bank of
Calcutta, opened in 1840.

24
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:33,036
It offered merchants advances
on goods to be imported,

25
00:01:33,116 --> 00:01:37,477
up to three-quarters of their
value at 9 per cent interest,

26
00:01:37,557 --> 00:01:42,150
and loans against bullion of up
to 90 per cent at 7 per cent.

27
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:44,892
It also issued the first banknotes with

28
00:01:44,972 --> 00:01:47,650
the word "Singapore" printed on them.

29
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:52,550
Its parent bank in
Calcutta collapsed in 1848.

30
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:55,441
A year before the Oriental Bank opened,

31
00:01:55,521 --> 00:02:00,698
The Straits Times of the 5th of
August 1845 reported that a Singapore

32
00:02:00,778 --> 00:02:04,660
branch was "in contemplation",
to complete the chain

33
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:08,625
of banking establishments
between Bombay and China.

34
00:02:08,625 --> 00:02:12,490
Other banks followed, each based
elsewhere and each opening a

35
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:16,450
Singapore branch for the trade
that passed through the port.

36
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:20,905
The Oriental Bank came in
1846, the Mercantile Bank

37
00:02:20,985 --> 00:02:25,085
of India in 1855, the
Dutch trading company the

38
00:02:25,165 --> 00:02:28,820
Nederlandsche Handel-Maatschappij in 1857,

39
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:34,325
and the Chartered Bank of India,
Australia and China in 1859.

40
00:02:34,325 --> 00:02:38,668
The Hongkong and Shanghai
Bank did business in Singapore

41
00:02:38,748 --> 00:02:43,248
through an agency from the
mid-1860s and, according to the

42
00:02:43,328 --> 00:02:48,225
bank's own history, opened its
first Singapore office in 1877.

43
00:02:48,225 --> 00:02:52,317
These were European banks, and
most of their Chinese customers

44
00:02:52,397 --> 00:02:56,287
reached them through a comprador,
a local intermediary who,

45
00:02:56,367 --> 00:03:01,198
in the National Library Board's words,
"was responsible for every Chinese

46
00:03:01,278 --> 00:03:05,450
account opened" and who also
recruited the bank's local staff.

47
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:08,845
The comprador vouched for
the customers he brought in,

48
00:03:08,925 --> 00:03:12,850
and the bank relied on his
judgement of who could be trusted.

49
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:17,236
Alongside the banks, many borrowers
who could not get a bank loan

50
00:03:17,316 --> 00:03:21,850
went to the Chettiars, professional
moneylenders from South India.

51
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:26,439
The National Library Board's account
describes how they drew capital

52
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:30,764
from wealthy investors in India,
from local depositors and from

53
00:03:30,844 --> 00:03:35,375
the European banks themselves,
which lent to them on demand notes.

54
00:03:35,375 --> 00:03:38,163
The banks found the
arrangement safe enough,

55
00:03:38,243 --> 00:03:43,000
because their losses in dealing with
the Chettiars were relatively small.

56
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,375
The Chettiars' terms could
be expensive for borrowers:

57
00:03:46,455 --> 00:03:51,045
according to the same account, interest
was charged on the full amount of

58
00:03:51,125 --> 00:03:54,756
the promissory note rather
than the sum actually received,

59
00:03:54,836 --> 00:03:59,250
and unpaid amounts were rolled over
into a new note at a higher rate.

60
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:04,100
The community's standing in the
town shows in the temple it built.

61
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:07,378
The Sri Thendayuthapani
Temple on Tank Road,

62
00:04:07,458 --> 00:04:11,728
better known as the Chettiars'
Temple, was built in 1859,

63
00:04:11,808 --> 00:04:15,700
the same year the Chartered
Bank opened its branch.

64
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,248
The foreign banks gave
Singapore's trade access to large

65
00:04:19,328 --> 00:04:23,458
amounts of capital and to London
and the other ports of the East,

66
00:04:23,538 --> 00:04:26,373
and by the end of the
century their buildings

67
00:04:26,453 --> 00:04:30,275
stood along Raffles Place,
Battery Road and the waterfront.

68
00:04:30,275 --> 00:04:32,708
But they did not replace the older

69
00:04:32,787 --> 00:04:35,300
ways of keeping and lending money.

70
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:39,708
The agency houses, the compradors
and the Chettiars all went on

71
00:04:39,788 --> 00:04:43,840
working alongside the banks,
and in the early 20th century

72
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:48,550
Singapore's own Chinese-owned
banks began to open alongside them.

73
00:04:48,550 --> 00:04:50,846
Where it fits in the bigger story:

74
00:04:50,926 --> 00:04:54,970
Singapore's financial history
is usually told from the bank

75
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:58,125
buildings of Raffles
Place and Battery Road.

76
00:04:58,125 --> 00:05:03,130
For the first two decades there were
none, and the port still ran on trust,

77
00:05:03,210 --> 00:05:07,075
credit and silver through
agency houses and moneylenders.

78
00:05:07,075 --> 00:05:11,139
When the banks came, from 1840,
they settled into that system,

79
00:05:11,219 --> 00:05:14,681
and the people who connected
them to their customers,

80
00:05:14,761 --> 00:05:16,886
the compradors and the Chettiars,

81
00:05:16,966 --> 00:05:20,575
are the part of the story
that is easiest to overlook.
